{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>הִתְפָּאֵר עָלַי.</b> As in ״הֲיִתְפָּאֵר הַגַּרְזֶן עַל הַחֹצֵב בּוֹ״ (Isaiah 10) — he boasts to say, 'I am greater than you'; ונטי\"ר בְּלַעַ\"ז. So too הִתְפָּאֵר עָלַי — he boasts to act clever and to ask a great thing, and to say that I cannot do it." "<b>לְמָתַי אַעְתִּיר לְךָ.</b> That which I pray for you today — for the cutting off of the frogs — when do you want them to be cut off? And you will see whether I fulfill my word at the deadline you set for me. If it had said מָתַי אַעְתִּיר, the meaning would be: when will I pray? Now that it says לְמָתַי, the meaning is: I pray for you today that the frogs be cut off at the time you set for me — tell me for which day you want them cut off. <b>אַעְתִּיר, הַעְתִּירוּ, וְהַעְתַרְתִּי.</b> And it is not said אֶעְתַּר, עִתְרוּ, וְעָתַרְתִּי, because every stem of עתר is a multiplicative of פלל; and just as one says אַרְבֶּה, הַרְבּוּ, וְהִרְבֵּיתִי — hif'il stem — so one says אַעְתִּיר, הַעְתִּירוּ, וְהַעְתַרְתִּי דְּבָרִים; and the root for all of them is ״וְהַעְתַּרְתֶּם עָלַי דִּבְרֵיכֶם״ (Ezekiel 35) — you multiplied."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {0 "the la'az is Old French vanter, 'to boast'."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:51:08.194226078Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}